# =============================================================================
# 09 — Entra ID app registration (secure the API)  (pairs with
#      docs/MANAGED_IDENTITY_ENTRA_ID.md)
# =============================================================================
#
# ⚠️  LEARNING REFERENCE — read it, run it yourself when ready. Assumes
#     $RG / $SUFFIX are set from 00-resource-group.azcli.
#
# 💰  COST — Entra ID app registrations are FREE. (The identity platform itself
#     is free at this tier; you only pay for premium AD features you won't use
#     here.)
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THEORY — App registration = your API's identity in Entra ID
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the INBOUND-auth half of docs/MANAGED_IDENTITY_ENTRA_ID.md: instead of
# the API managing its own usernames/passwords, Entra ID issues callers a JWT,
# and the API just VALIDATES it (signature, audience, issuer, expiry). To do
# that the API needs to be REGISTERED in Entra ID so it has:
#   * an Application (client) ID  -> becomes AzureAd:ClientId (the "audience"
#     the token must be addressed to),
#   * a Tenant ID                 -> becomes AzureAd:TenantId (whose tokens to
#     trust),
# both of which Program.cs reads to switch on AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi. Set
# neither and the [Authorize] endpoints simply have no auth scheme wired up
# (the conditional guard in Program.cs).
#
# NOTE: this is DIFFERENT from the Web App's Managed Identity in 03. That
# identity is the app authenticating OUTBOUND to Azure (Key Vault etc.). This
# registration is about validating callers coming INBOUND to the app. Same
# platform, opposite directions — the single most useful distinction to have
# straight for interviews.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

APP_REG_NAME="HighFidelity-Api-$SUFFIX"

# --- 1. Register the API as an application -----------------------------------
# --identifier-uris gives the API a unique "audience" URI that tokens target.
az ad app create \
  --display-name "$APP_REG_NAME" \
  --identifier-uris "api://highfidelity-$SUFFIX"

# --- 2. Read back the IDs the app config needs -------------------------------
CLIENT_ID=$(az ad app list --display-name "$APP_REG_NAME" --query "[0].appId" -o tsv)
TENANT_ID=$(az account show --query tenantId -o tsv)
echo "AzureAd__ClientId=$CLIENT_ID"
echo "AzureAd__TenantId=$TENANT_ID"
echo "AzureAd__Instance=https://login.microsoftonline.com/"
# Set those three as app settings on the Web App (03 step 4). Once present,
# /api/secure/whoami starts requiring a valid Entra ID token.

# --- 3. (Optional) expose a scope, so clients can request a token ------------
# For a client to actually GET a token for this API you'd also define an
# exposed API scope (e.g. "access_as_user") and register a client app that
# requests it. That's several more steps and is usually done in the portal's
# "Expose an API" / "API permissions" blades — out of scope for this quick
# reference, but that's the direction: define scope -> client requests token
# for api://highfidelity-<suffix>/access_as_user -> sends it as a Bearer header.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERIFY:
#   Without a token:  GET https://<webapp>/api/secure/whoami  -> 401 Unauthorized
#   With a valid Entra ID bearer token for this app's audience -> 200 + your
#   token's claims echoed back. Getting a real token requires step 3's scope +
#   a client, which is why this topic is most often EXPLAINED rather than fully
#   exercised end-to-end (see docs/MANAGED_IDENTITY_ENTRA_ID.md).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
